The pattern is symmetric around its widest row. This code builds one row as a string and prints it, so the top and bottom halves use the same small piece of logic.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern6(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= 2 * n - 1; row++) {
            int width = row <= n ? row : 2 * n - row;
            int spaces = n - width;

            StringBuilder line = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
                line.append(' ');
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < 2 * width - 1; i++) {
                line.append('*');
            }

            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

A quick dry run with `n = 2` gives widths `1, 2, 1`, so the output is one star, then three stars, then one star. There is no separate downward loop because the width formula handles both halves.

The code assumes `n` is positive. For `n = 1`, it prints just one star.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current row string.

The recursive helper prints the growing half first, then prints the smaller rows while returning from the calls. It is a different way to represent the same symmetry, although the loop versions use less memory.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern6(int n) {
        printRows(n, 1);
    }

    private void printRows(int n, int width) {
        if (width > 2 * n - 1) {
            return;
        }

        int rowWidth = width <= 2 * n - 1 ? width : 0;
        int spaces = (2 * n - 1 - rowWidth) / 2;
        printLine(rowWidth, spaces);
        printRows(n, width + 2);

        if (width != 2 * n - 1) {
            printLine(rowWidth, spaces);
        }
    }

    private void printLine(int width, int spaces) {
        for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
            System.out.print(' ');
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < width; i++) {
            System.out.print('*');
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

The recursive calls create widths `1, 3, 5, ...` and then print those rows again as the calls finish, except for the widest row. With `n = 3`, this gives `1, 3, 5, 3, 1`.

The base case stops after the widest row. Recursion makes this shorter to describe, but it uses call-stack space, so an iterative version is safer for very large `n`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

I used two parts for the diamond: first the rows grow, then they shrink. Each row prints spaces first and stars after that. The number of stars follows `2 * row - 1`, so the middle row is the widest one.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern6(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
                cout << ' ';
            }
            for (int star = 1; star <= 2 * row - 1; star++) {
                cout << '*';
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }

        for (int row = n - 1; row >= 1; row--) {
            for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
                cout << ' ';
            }
            for (int star = 1; star <= 2 * row - 1; star++) {
                cout << '*';
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 3`, the star counts are `1, 3, 5, 3, 1`, which makes the diamond shape. The input is only used as the number of rows in the top half.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the middle row can contain about `2n` stars.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

This version keeps the printing of one centered row in a lambda. The main loop only decides how far each row is from the center, which avoids writing nearly identical code for the two halves.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern6(int n) {
        auto printRow = [](int width, int spaces) {
            for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
                cout << ' ';
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < width; i++) {
                cout << '*';
            }
            cout << '\n';
        };

        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            printRow(2 * row - 1, n - row);
        }
        for (int row = n - 1; row >= 1; row--) {
            printRow(2 * row - 1, n - row);
        }
    }
};
```

Dry run for `n = 4`: the top half has widths `1, 3, 5, 7`, and the second half repeats `5, 3, 1`. The middle row is printed only once because the second loop starts at `n - 1`.

For a non-positive value, the loops print nothing. Under the usual pattern constraints, `n` is at least 1.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the output and the small lambda object.

